{#
    Blog filters — the in-place alternative to nav.twig.

    nav.twig links out to the topic pages, which are real indexable URLs
    (topics/{slug}). This filters the current listing instead, which is
    nicer to use but produces query-string URLs — and those are noindex under
    config/stables/seo.php's robots rule, deliberately, so they can't compete with the
    topic pages.

    That means the two aren't equivalent and shouldn't both be shown: pick one
    with useFilters in index.twig. Filters for a browsing-first site, nav where
    the topic pages are meant to rank. Separately, the posts block's Show
    Filters field hides whichever one is picked.

    Same approach as the events filters: a plain GET form, native controls,
    works with JavaScript off. Expects `topics`, plus the already-validated
    `selectedTopic` / `keyword` and `baseUrl` from index.twig.
#}
{% set hasFilters = selectedTopic or keyword %}

<div class="blog-filters section relative">
    <div class="blog-filters__int container">
        <form method="get" class="blog-filters__form" aria-labelledby="blog-filters-legend">
            <fieldset class="blog-filters__set">
                <legend id="blog-filters-legend" class="visually-hidden">{{ 'Filter posts'|t }}</legend>

                {% if topics|length %}
                <div class="blog-filters__field">
                    <label for="blog-topic">{{ 'Topic'|t }}</label>
                    <select id="blog-topic" name="topic" class="blog-filters__select">
                        <option value="">{{ 'All topics'|t }}</option>
                        {% for topic in topics %}
                            <option value="{{ topic.slug }}" {{ topic.slug == selectedTopic ? 'selected' }}>{{ topic.title }}</option>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </select>
                </div>
                {% endif %}

                <div class="blog-filters__field">
                    <label for="blog-keyword">{{ 'Search'|t }}</label>
                    {# type=search gets the platform's clear button and the
                       right on-screen keyboard on mobile, for free. #}
                    <input type="search" id="blog-keyword" name="q" value="{{ keyword }}"
                           class="blog-filters__input" maxlength="120">
                </div>

                <div class="blog-filters__actions">
                    <button type="submit" class="btn blog-filters__submit">{{ 'Filter'|t }}</button>
                    {% if hasFilters %}
                        <a href="{{ baseUrl }}" class="btn btn--text blog-filters__reset">{{ 'Clear'|t }}</a>
                    {% endif %}
                </div>
            </fieldset>
        </form>
    </div>
</div>
